9 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157299 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157300 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1045 "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157490 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b01ba0f8-312d-5711-866c-f427e34e9a49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157834 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157839 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/dfaa18cb3295d3bb1641f440a3c1642758fe793c3605cf7d010121a68bbff47d.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "932bfdfe-cb1f-5acf-bcb0-ab10a83a0361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157840 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/46/4660d61851814c87afc7c140dd87e1354785f55cafe4fbc911fd369f768914e3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{4-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-\\frac{1}{3}(4-{r}^{2})^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-3\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1061 27487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0aaacbc4-1148-50d7-a5cf-50570ae0a925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158141 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1061 27488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6d6c9e7-5ab7-5550-b9f5-44b7f15426b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158142 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1061 27489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "659f011d-8169-5c22-b20a-0f19271882cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158143 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1061 27493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd3a2583-f9bf-5dcc-9d80-5572b021e950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1061 27531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "098b5c2c-68e3-5938-bf5f-621a688c8ba7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\n             \n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1065 4466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26614628-ce8b-5afe-ac90-b277c7c0f887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158486 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's get some more practice at expressing regions in polar coordinates. \n\nConsider the region enclosed by the curve $x^2+(y-2)^2=4$ and the line $y=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dffcd94cc96c33d173704a63cb03fb0f0098bc67d6c66a855c16f59694bde8e1.png)\n\nThis region represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, we recall the usual transformations from polar to Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the functions that bound our region in polar coordinates:\n\n- For $x^2+(y-2)^2 = 4,$  we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 4\\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ \n\nFirst of all, note that if we draw an arrow through the region from the origin, it enters the region via the curve $r=2\\csc\\theta$ and exits via $r=4\\sin\\theta \\mathbin{:}$  \n\n![](images/b0/b09ab4e4e08dfb056964947dc866b965940b761d70c945faf001446c1df7869f.png)\n\nSecondly, our curves intersect at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/6a/6a132d5f8c58ce60c794595ee80c57c5b9b02e630004808cd53a7677efd022e7.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 2\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq 4\\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\Big\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1065 4472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "870d0884-b353-5292-b2e5-593725024502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158487 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351575 #edb/ref 1080 "2-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351575 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351575 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "013b7b16-b535-5412-8ea8-edd8fe941911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351611 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351611