#edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424326 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424326 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424326 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc66dd31-0a57-5231-b848-a0871bc42f3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45cc9fce-954c-547e-835b-7dec4ec6d636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424327 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(9-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78459587-308f-5867-9175-f728a41ccebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424328 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/49/4903ca2ba8cde3f7874e10998a0741035ec7b2dea69a7804eaca64cb552fc572.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2